    1. Port Scanning (SYN Scanning / Reconnaissance)

Theory: An attacker sends mass SYN packets to various ports on a target host to discover active services and 
vulnerabilities.
Network Marker: A massive flood of SYN packets from a single external IP to many different destination ports, 
resulting in either RST-ACK (port closed) or no response (timeout).

tcpdump Command:
tcpdump -nn -r traffic.pcap "tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0" | awk '{print $3, $5}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 10

Wireshark Filter:
tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0

    2. DNS Tunneling & C2 Traffic

Theory: Attackers bypass firewalls by encoding stolen data or malware commands inside standard DNS queries sent to a
rogue authoritative name server.
Network Marker: A massive spike in UDP 53 requests to a specific domain, where the subdomains are unusually long,
high-entropy strings of random characters (e.g., ://malicious.com).

tcpdump Command:
tcpdump -nn -r traffic.pcap udp port 53 -A | grep -E -o "[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}" | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 10

Wireshark Filter:
dns.flags.response == 0 && dns.qry.name.len > 30

    3. Command & Control Beaconing (C2 Beaconing)

Theory: Infected hosts (bots/trojans) check in with the attacker's server to receive new instructions at strict,
automated intervals.
Network Marker: Perfect mathematical periodicity in the packet timestamps 
(e.g., traffic leaves exactly every 60 seconds or every 5 minutes). Humans do not generate traffic with 
clockwork precision.

tcpdump Command: (Filters traffic to a specific suspicious IP to check timestamp patterns manually)
tcpdump -nn -r traffic.pcap host [suspicious_ip] | awk '{print $1}' | head -n 20

Wireshark Filter: (Isolates a single conversation to analyze the "Time delta from previous displayed packet" column)
ip.addr == [suspicious_ip] && tcp.flags.syn == 1

    4. Data Exfiltration (Abnormal Outbound Data)

Theory: An attacker has compromised a database or file system and is transferring massive amounts of sensitive data 
out of the network.
Network Marker: Web and DB traffic are normally highly asymmetrical—requests are small, responses are large. During
exfiltration, the internal host transmits significantly more data than it receives (Data Out > Data In).

tcpdump Command: (Finds packets with a payload size larger than 1000 bytes leaving your network)
tcpdump -nn -r traffic.pcap -v "src net 192.168.1.0/24 and not dst net 192.168.1.0/24" | grep -E "length [1-9][0-9]{3,}" | head -n 10

Wireshark Filter:
ip.src == 192.168.1.0/24 && !ip.dst == 192.168.1.0/24 && tcp.len > 1000

    5. SYN Flood (DDoS Attack)

Theory: An attacker attempts to crash a server by flooding it with spoofed (fake) TCP connection requests, overwhelming
the server's connection queue.
Network Marker: Thousands of inbound SYN packets per second. The server responds with SYN-ACK, but the final ACK from 
the client never arrives, leaving the sockets stuck in the SYN_RECV state.

tcpdump Command: (Counts total SYN packets in the file to gauge the scale of the flood)
tcpdump -nn -r traffic.pcap "tcp[tcpflags] & (tcp-syn) != 0" | wc -l

Wireshark Filter:
tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0
``` *(Tip: Go to Statistics -> Conversations -> TCP to look for thousands of incomplete sessions).*

    6. Cleartext Administrative Protocols

Theory: Legitimate admins or attackers use obsolete, unencrypted protocols, allowing anyone on the path to sniff 
credentials or inject commands.
Network Marker: Traffic active on ports 21 (FTP), 23 (Telnet), or 80 (HTTP).

tcpdump Command: (Reads the packet payloads in ASCII format directly on the screen to look for passwords)
tcpdump -nn -A -r traffic.pcap port 23 or port 21
tcpdump -nn -A -r traffic.pcap "portrange 21-23 or port 80"
tcpdump -nn -A -r traffic.pcap port 80 | grep -iE "password|pwd|login|user|post"


Wireshark Filter:
telnet || ftp || http
``` *(Tip: Right-click a packet and choose "Follow -> TCP Stream" to read the conversation).*

    7. ARP Spoofing (Man-in-the-Middle / MitM)
Theory: An attacker in the local network broadcasts fraudulent ARP messages to associate their MAC address with the
IP address of the default gateway (router) or a target victim.
Network Marker: A sudden surge of unsolicited ARP replies. You will see one physical MAC address claiming ownership 
over multiple different IP addresses in the network log.

tcpdump Command:
tcpdump -nn -r traffic.pcap arp

Wireshark Filter:
arp.duplicate-address-detected || arp.opcode == 2

    8. Authentication Brute-Forcing
Theory: Automated tools repeatedly attempt to guess passwords on exposed services like SSH (22), RDP (3389), or web 
login portals.
Network Marker: Hundreds or thousands of TCP connections opened and torn down within seconds between the same two IPs. 
The sessions terminate abruptly after the server sends an authentication failure response.

tcpdump Command: (Counts connection attempts to the SSH port grouped by source IP)
tcpdump -nn -r traffic.pcap dst port 22 and "tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0" | awk '{print $3}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr

Wireshark Filter:
tcp.dstport == 22 && tcp.flags.syn == 1

    9. Web Directory Busting (Web Reconnaissance)
Theory: Attackers use automated fuzzers (e.g., Dirbuster, Gobuster) to brute-force URLs looking for hidden admin
panels, backups, or unlinked directories.
Network Marker: An intense stream of HTTP GET requests to highly varied paths (/admin, /backup.sql, /secret/), 
resulting in a massive volume of server responses showing 404 Not Found or 403 Forbidden.

tcpdump Command: (Searches for HTTP 404 response strings inside web traffic)
tcpdump -nn -A -r traffic.pcap tcp port 80 | grep "HTTP/1.1 404" -B 2 -A 5

Wireshark Filter:
http.response.code == 404 || http.response.code == 403

    10. Abnormal or Malformed TCP Flags (Evasion Scans)
Theory: Attackers craft packets with combinations of TCP flags that violate official network standards (RFCs) in an
attempt to bypass primitive firewall logic or fingerprint the OS.
Network Marker: Packets containing nonsensical flag combinations like Xmas (FIN+PSH+URG set simultaneously)
or Null (all flags set to 0).

tcpdump Command: (Finds Null scans where the TCP flag byte equals 0)
tcpdump -nn -r traffic.pcap "tcp[tcpflags] == 0"

Wireshark Filter:
tcp.flags == 0x000



